From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: valentin rameau <valentin.rameau@outlook.fr>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 07:09:37 +0900
Subject: [PATCH 27/33] PPU: record the winning OAM slot per pixel

The sprite pass already resolves a unique winner per pixel -- lower OAM
indices are processed later and overwrite higher ones -- but throws the
identity away, so a host that wants to know which sprite covered a pixel
has to re-render the scanline once per OAM range it cares about.

One byte store beside the existing dst[0] write keeps it. Read objSlotOf
only where objBuffer's low byte is non-zero: the backdrop clear leaves
0x0500 and every sprite store carries a non-zero palette index, so that
byte is what distinguishes a pixel this line wrote from a stale one, and
no per-line clear of the array is needed.

Measured on the desktop build, race steady state: 3,558 stores/frame
(max 7,433), so the cost on the native path is on the order of 16 us on
a 444 MHz Cortex-A9.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 runner/src/snes/ppu.c | 1 +
 runner/src/snes/ppu.h | 7 +++++++
 2 files changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/runner/src/snes/ppu.c b/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
index c0581f2..83f9b0e 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
+++ b/runner/src/snes/ppu.c
@@ -2745,6 +2745,7 @@ static bool ppu_evaluateSprites(Ppu* ppu, int line) {
               }
         // Lower OAM indices are processed later and overwrite higher ones.
                 dst[0] = z + pixel;
+                ppu->objSlotOf[dst - ppu->objBuffer.data] = (uint8_t)slot;
             }
         }
         if(tilesFound > 34 &&
diff --git a/runner/src/snes/ppu.h b/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
index 45f9b75..9946673 100644
--- a/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
+++ b/runner/src/snes/ppu.h
@@ -314,6 +314,13 @@ struct Ppu {
   uint32_t renderFlags;
   PpuPixelPrioBufs bgBuffers[2];
   PpuPixelPrioBufs objBuffer;
+  /* Winning OAM slot per pixel of objBuffer, for the line just drawn. Read it
+   * only where objBuffer's low byte is non-zero: the backdrop clear writes
+   * 0x0500 and every sprite store carries a non-zero palette index, so a
+   * non-zero low byte is what says this line wrote both. It lets a host answer
+   * "which sprite covered this pixel" without re-rendering the line once per
+   * OAM range. Host-only derived state, never emulated state. */
+  uint8_t objSlotOf[kPpuBufWidth];
   /* Per-source isolated priority pixels for generic host-overlay captures. */
   PpuPixelPrioBufs overlayBuffers[kPpuOverlaySource_Count];
   PpuOverlayCapture overlayCaptures[kPpuOverlaySource_Count];
